":1865.9397260273972,"title":"King of the Belgians","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Leopold I of Belgium"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q12973","value_label":"Leopold III of Belgium","from_t":1934.145205479452,"to_t":1951.5369863013698,"title":"King of the Belgians","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Leopold III of Belgium"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q12976","value_label":"Baudouin I of Belgium","from_t":1951.5397260273974,"to_t":1993.5780821917808,"title":"King of the Belgians","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Baudouin I of Belgium"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q3911","value_label":"Albert II of Belgium","from_t":1993.6027397260275,"to_t":2013.5506849315068,"title":"King of the Belgians","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Albert II of Belgium"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q55008046","value_label":"Albert I of Belgium","from_t":1909.9753424657533,"to_t":1934.1287671232876,"title":"King of the Belgians","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Albert I of Belgium"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q1079522","value_label":"Érasme-Louis Surlet de Chokier","from_t":1831.1479452054793,"to_t":1831.5506849315068,"title":"Regent of Belgium","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Érasme-Louis Surlet de Chokier"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q445553","value_label":"Prince Charles, Count of Flanders","from_t":1944.7185792349726,"to_t":1950.5506849315068,"title":"Regent of Belgium","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Prince Charles, Count of Flanders"},{"qid":"Q31","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q12990","value_label":"Flag of Belgium (civil).svg","from_t":1831.7780821917809,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Belgium","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Belgium"}],"people":{"Q155004":{"label":"Philippe of Belgium","description":"King of the Belgians since 2013","enwiki":"Philippe of Belgium","summary":"Philippe Léopold Louis Marie (born 15 April 1960) is  King of the Belgians, reigning since 21 July 2013.\nHe is the eldest child of King Albert II and Queen Paola. He succeeded his father upon the former's abdication for health reasons on 21 July 2013. He married Mathilde d'Udekem d'Acoz in 1999, with whom he has four children.","image":"Visit of King Philippe I of Belgium to Ghent European Youth Capital on 25 April 2024 - EU2024BE 240425 Visit of His Majesty the King to Ghent European Youth Capital Ghent JNZT 0078 (53677952391).jpg","birth":1960.2868852459017,"death":null},"Q336599":{"label":"Bart De Wever","description":"Prime Minister at Government of Belgium","enwiki":"Bart De Wever","summary":"Bart Albert Liliane De Wever (Dutch: [ˈbɑrt də ˈʋeːvər]; born 21 December 1970), also known as BDW, is a Belgian politician who has served as the Prime Minister of Belgium since February 2025. From 2004 to 2025, De Wever had been the leader of the New Flemish Alliance (N-VA), a political party advocating Flemish independence. From January 2013 to February 2025, he was Mayor of Antwerp, following the 2012 municipal elections.\nDe Wever presided over his party's victory in the 2010 federal elections when N-VA became the largest party in both Flanders and in Belgium as a whole.","image":"Belgian Prime Minister Bart De Wever (head + shoulders).jpg","birth":1970.9698630136986,"death":null},"Q12967":{"label":"Leopold II of Belgium","description":"King of the Belgians from 1865 to 1909 (1835–1909)","enwiki":"Leopold II of Belgium","summary":"Leopold II (9 April 1835 – 17 December 1909) was the second king of the Belgians from 1865 to 1909 as well as sovereign of the Congo Free State from 1885 to 1908.\nBorn in Brussels as the second but eldest-surviving son of King Leopold I and Queen Louise, Leopold succeeded his father to the Belgian throne in 1865 and reigned for 44 years until his death, the longest reign of a Belgian monarch to date. He died without surviving legitimate sons; the current king of the Belgians, Philippe, descends from his nephew and successor, Albert I. He is popularly referred to as the Builder King in Belgium in reference to the great number of buildings, urban projects and public works he commissioned.\nLeopold was the founder and sole owner of the Congo Free State, a private colonial project undertaken on his own behalf as a personal union with Belgium.","image":"Leopold ii garter knight.jpg","birth":1835.268493150685,"death":1909.958904109589},"Q12971":{"label":"Leopold I of Belgium","description":"German prince who became the first King of the Belgians (1790–1865)","enwiki":"Leopold I of Belgium","summary":"Leopold I (Leopold George Christian Frederick; 16 December 1790 – 10 December 1865) was the first king of the Belgians, reigning from 21 July 1831 until his death in 1865.\nThe youngest son of Francis, Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld, Leopold took a commission in the Imperial Russian Army and fought against Napoleon after French troops overran Saxe-Coburg during the Napoleonic Wars. After Napoleon's defeat, Leopold moved to the United Kingdom, where in 1816 he married Princess Charlotte of Wales, the only child of the British Prince Regent (later George IV). Leopold and Charlotte's marriage was happy, but it ended after a year and a half when Charlotte died after delivering a stillborn son.","image":"1840 portrait of King Leopold I (King of the Belgians) by Winterhalter.jpg","birth":1790.9561643835616,"death":1865.9397260273972},"Q12973":{"label":"Leopold III of Belgium","description":"king of the Belgians from 1934 to 1951 (1901–1983)","enwiki":"Leopold III of Belgium","summary":"Leopold III (3 November 1901 – 25 September 1983) was King of the Belgians from 23 February 1934 until his abdication on 16 July 1951. At the outbreak of World War II, Leopold tried to maintain Belgian neutrality, but after the German invasion in May 1940, he surrendered his country, earning him much hostility, both at home and abroad.\nLeopold's act was declared unconstitutional by Prime Minister Hubert Pierlot and his cabinet, who moved to London to form a government-in-exile, while Leopold and his family were placed under house arrest by the Germans. In 1944, they were moved to Germany and then Austria, before being liberated by the Americans, but banned for some years from returning to Belgium, where his brother Prince Charles, Count of Flanders, had been declared regent.","image":"Leopold III (1934).jpg","birth":1901.8383561643836,"death":1983.731506849315},"Q12976":{"label":"Baudouin I of Belgium","description":"fifth king of the Belgians (1930–1993)","enwiki":"Baudouin of Belgium","summary":"Baudouin (US: ; 7 September 1930 – 31 July 1993) was King of the Belgians from 17 July 1951 until his death in 1993. He was the last Belgian king to be sovereign of the Congo, before it became independent in 1960 and became the Democratic Republic of the Congo (known from 1971 to 1997 as Zaire).\nBaudouin was the elder son of King Leopold III (1901–1983) and his first wife, Princess Astrid of Sweden (1905–1935). Because he and his wife, Queen Fabiola, had no children, at Baudouin's death the crown passed to his younger brother, King Albert II.\nIn 2024, the Holy See opened the cause for his beatification, granting him the title Servant of God.","image":"King Baudouin of Belgium.jpg","birth":1930.682191780822,"death":1993.5780821917808},"Q3911":{"label":"Albert II of Belgium","description":"sixth king of the Belgians","enwiki":"Albert II of Belgium","summary":"Albert II (born 6 June 1934) is a member of the Belgian royal family who reigned as King of the Belgians from 1993 until his abdication in 2013.\nAlbert II is the son of King Leopold III and the last living child of Queen Astrid, born a princess of Sweden. He is the younger brother of the late Grand Duchess Joséphine-Charlotte of Luxembourg and King Baudouin, whom he succeeded following Baudouin's death in 1993. He married Donna Paola Ruffo di Calabria (now Queen Paola), with whom he had three children.","image":"Albert II of Belgium at the 17th session of the EEC-ACP Joint Assembly (cropped).jpg","birth":1934.427397260274,"death":null},"Q55008046":{"label":"Albert I of Belgium","description":"King of the Belgians from 1909 to 1934 (1875–1934)","enwiki":"Albert I of Belgium","summary":"Albert I (8 April 1875 – 17 February 1934) was King of the Belgians from 23 December 1909 until his death in 1934. He is popularly referred to as the Knight King (Dutch: Koning-Ridder; French: Roi-Chevalier) or Soldier King (Dutch: Koning-Soldaat; French: Roi-Soldat) in Belgium in reference to his role during World War I.\nAlbert was born in Brussels as the fifth child and second son of Prince Philippe, Count of Flanders, and Princess Marie of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen. He succeeded his uncle Leopold II to the Belgian throne in 1909. He married Elisabeth of Bavaria, with whom he had three children.","image":"Albert I Koning der Belgen.jpg","birth":1875.2657534246575,"death":1934.1287671232876},"Q1079522":{"label":"Érasme-Louis Surlet de Chokier","description":"Belgian politician (1769-1839)","enwiki":"Érasme-Louis Surlet de Chokier","summary":"Érasme-Louis Surlet de Chokier (French pronunciation: [eʁasm lwi syʁlɛ də ʃɔkje]; 27 November 1769 – 7 August 1839) was a Belgian politician who served as the first Regent of Belgium from 24 February 1831 until 21 July 1831, prior to the accession of Leopold I of Belgium.","image":"Surletdechokier.jpg","birth":1769.9041095890411,"death":1839.5972602739726},"Q445553":{"label":"Prince Charles, Count of Flanders","description":"prince, painter (1903-1983)","enwiki":"Prince Charles, Count of Flanders","summary":"Prince Charles, Count of Flanders (Dutch: Karel, German: Karl; 10 October 1903 – 1 June 1983) was a member of the Belgian royal family who served as regent of Belgium from 1944 until 1950, while a judicial commission investigated his elder brother, King Leopold III of Belgium, as to whether he betrayed the Allies of World War II by an allegedly premature surrender in 1940 and collaboration with the Nazis during the occupation of Belgium. Charles' regency ended when Leopold was allowed to return to Belgium. Shortly after returning and resuming his monarchical duties, Leopold abdicated in favour of his son, Baudouin.\nDuring the Second World War, Charles was known as General du Boc, in order to hide his identity for security reasons.","image":"Karel van België Charles de Belgique Karl von Belgien.jpg","birth":1903.772602739726,"death":1983.4136986301369}},"media":{"Flag of Belgium.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Flag_of_Belgium.svg/330px-Flag_of_Belgium.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belgium.svg","author":"Dbenbenn and others","license":"Public domain"},"Great coat of arms of Belgium.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Great_coat_of_arms_of_Belgium.svg/330px-Great_coat_of_arms_of_Belgium.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_coat_of_arms_of_Belgium.svg","author":"Sodacan, with some elements by Katepanomegas","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Flag of Belgium (civil).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Flag_of_Belgium_%28civil%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Belgium_%28civil%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belgium_(civil).svg","author":"Dbenbenn","license":"Public domain"},"Visit of King Philippe I of Belgium to Ghent European Youth Capital on 25 April 2024 - EU2024BE 240425 Visit of His Majesty the King to Ghent European Youth Capital Ghent JNZT 0078 (53677952391).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Visit_of_King_Phil